1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
 Sous-titrage Société Radio-Canada

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,600
 Thank you for your question, I don't have the precise amount with regards to paving street parking lanes, but with regards to your intervention about parking on the street, I think it's just really important to recognise the fact that people that drive can also be bikers, and they can also be pedestrians, so it's very limited to position people in a box in that way.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,160
 So, I use the Burette bike path to cross the Decaire each way every workday as I bring my kids to school and daycare on a cargo bike.

4
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:16,560
 I am anxious about what will happen after November 15, when the bike path will become parking again for the winter.

5
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:23,640
 I expect that I'll have to cross at Van Horn or Isabella between cars rushing using every available space to squeeze around each other.

6
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:30,540
 These intersections are known to be extremely dangerous, just again to subsidise the auto-fossil fuel complex.

7
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:34,040
 Why is the Burette bike path not winterised?

8
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:36,760
 Thank you so much for your question.

9
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:44,460
 That was actually a decision that was made by the previous administration, and at this time, we are evaluating the entire network.

10
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:51,180
 Like I said in my previous intervention, if we want to add bike paths to the network, which is really important that we do,

11
00:01:51,840 --> 00:02:00,820
 I think that we have to have a more comprehensive look at the current network and see how we can improve it.

12
00:02:00,820 --> 00:02:06,700
 And so we're doing that work with our colleagues here, and we're also trying to work with our other neighbours,

13
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:10,520
 whether it's Hampstead, Montreal West, Côte Saint-Luc, Ville Mont-Royal.

14
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:17,980
 We are a very central borough, and we're surrounded by demerged municipalities that we have to work with

15
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:21,260
 if we want to have the connectivity that we hope to have in terms of mobility.

16
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:22,480
 Thank you for your question.

17
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:23,080
 Thank you.

18
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:24,600
 Good night.

19
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:29,260
 Lisa Freeman.

20
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:40,000
 Thank you.

21
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:45,380
 My first question is quite straightforward, and it's around snow clearing.

22
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:52,100
 So I'd like to know what the new approach to snow clearing will be in order to prioritize inclusive mobility.

23
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:59,220
 Given the mention of different equipment in the last couple meetings, as well as some media around switching to more salt,

24
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:05,000
 I'd like to know the approach for bus stops, sidewalks, curb cuts, crosswalks, and bike lanes.

25
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:08,400
 Last season, there were many times when the wind rose,

26
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:11,340
 the piles of snow after plowing the road,

27
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:15,540
 blocked the curb cut, the transition from the sidewalk to the intersection,

28
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:20,000
 or blocked the sidewalk itself so that bus stops could not be used,

29
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:25,340
 and narrowed the sidewalk so that people with wheelchairs, strollers, et cetera, couldn't use it.

30
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:28,620
 Thank you so much for coming once again to our council, Ms. Freeman.

31
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:36,840
 We're certainly not the administration that's going to say that the previous practices that existed in the City of Montreal or the borough are perfect whatsoever.

32
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:44,040
 I think that there's clearly an improvement that needs to be made with regards to snow clearing in general.

33
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:48,760
 We, as you probably know, we are working on our accessibility plan.

34
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:51,160
 There's a lot of recommendations that were made in that.

35
00:03:51,340 --> 00:03:57,500
 And as soon as it's adopted, one of the first actions that we're going to have is a committee on universal accessibility

36
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:02,660
 that will be sharing a lot of their experience with us so that we can optimize the work that we do.

37
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:12,300
 I'm going to pass it to our services with regards to the season so that we can clearly point out how we're going to improve on our snow clearing,

38
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:14,240
 especially when it comes to sidewalks.

39
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:24,380
 Écoutez, pardon, tout d'abord, en ce qui a trait peut-être précisé qu'au niveau des débarcadères,

40
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:28,680
 on comprend que, bon, depuis deux ans, les méthodes ont été modifiées.

41
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:39,160
 Maintenant, on a une approche où on vient cibler directement les débarcadères avec des équipements bien spécifiques

42
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:45,180
 pour permettre justement l'accès aux véhicules spécialisés.

43
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:54,360
 En ce qui a trait aux trottoirs, une approche différente cette année où, oui, il est annoncé qu'il y aura plus de sel sur les trottoirs.

44
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:56,780
 Évidemment, tout dépendra des conditions climatiques.

45
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:04,820
 C'est peut-être une question d'enchaînement de conditions, que ce soit au niveau du verglas, au niveau de la neige et de la pluie.

46
00:05:06,220 --> 00:05:13,660
 Et au niveau des équipements, bien, il y a certains ajouts d'équipements que nous aurons.

47
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:19,200
 Également, on doit quand même travailler également avec les équipements qui sont sur place.

48
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:32,580
 Donc, oui, une approche au niveau des trottoirs pour s'assurer d'une meilleure mobilité pour l'ensemble des usagers des trottoirs.

49
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:37,140
 Mais il reste qu'évidemment, ça prend le temps nécessaire pour le faire également.

50
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:39,360
 Would you like me to translate it?

51
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:39,720
 It's okay.

52
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:40,480
 Okay, perfect.

53
00:05:40,660 --> 00:05:40,900
 Merci.

54
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:45,580
 Okay, my second question has a little bit more of a preamble.

55
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:58,600
 So, I'd like to know how the borough will encourage, require, or compel inclusion of all, for example, through universal design and universal accessibility in your activities and your funding.

56
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:12,040
 And specifically, going through the program for this meeting, there were four instances where you could have very much worked on equity and inclusion, and it seems you did not or you chose not to.

57
00:06:12,420 --> 00:06:15,140
 So, I'll explain that a bit so you can best respond.

58
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:37,380
 So, for example, on page 148 and 164, in regards to the request for $10,000 of funding for the village de Fête on Monkland, there is a mention of equity and inclusion, but there is no mention of how it will be universally accessible or how businesses will be required to be accessible to all.

59
00:06:37,780 --> 00:06:48,740
 For example, to have a ramp if they have stairs, and there is no mention of how the money the borough gives of public funding will be there to support everybody in the public.

60
00:06:48,980 --> 00:07:04,720
 As the second example, there is a proposal to reduce inclusive mobility on page 207 by closing bike paths, which people have already talked about, and the section on climate impact and equity inclusion are blank.

61
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:13,080
 And I would assume that those would be very negative on inclusion and equity, and it seemed to have been chosen not to address that.

62
00:07:14,180 --> 00:07:23,340
 It also seemed to be sort of in contravention of the article of the Charter 24D that was cited, so that could be an opportunity.

63
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:30,000
 For the third one, the development on Elmhurst, page 329, there's no mention specifically

